ner_results: Mr. ***** is a 73 y.o. male who comes to our GI Oncology practice at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center in follow up for his recently diagnosed <1>pancreatic adenocarcinoma</1>. The history was obtained from the patient, along with my review of all his outside records. History of Present Illness, reviewed: This pleasant gentleman, previously quite healthy overall, originally presented to St. *****'s Hospital on 02/04/22 with the subacute onset of <2>painless jaundice</2> and <3>orange urine</3> developing over the preceding week. Initial <4>CT abdomen</4> (02/04/2022) was suspicious for a <5>pancreatic head mass</5> versus an <6>enlarged lymph node</6> along the portacaval region measuring at least 4.5 x 1.8 cm, with associated <7>moderate extrahepatic duct dilatation</7>. He was admitted to expedite work-up which to date has included: - <8>Bloodwork</8> on 02/05/22 significant for <9>ALT 972</9>, <10>AST 478</10>, <11>total bilirubin 11.2</11> - 02/05/22: Repeat <12>CT A/P</12> showed a <13>lobulated mass</13> at the <14>pancreatic head</14>, with either extension of the mass or an <15>enlarged lymph node</15> in the portacaval region. <16>Obstruction of the extrahepatic bile duct</16> was noted at the level of the pancreatic mass with <17>moderate intrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation</17> was also seen. - 02/10/22: <18>EUS</18>: <19>Irregular hypoechoic mass</19> was identified in the <20>pancreatic head</20> measuring 35 mm by 30 mm with well-defined endosonographic borders. An intact interface was seen between the mass and the adjacent structures suggesting a lack of invasion. One <21>enlarged lymph node</21> was visualized in the aortocaval region as well measuring 40 mm by 15 mm in maximal cross-sectional diameter. Findings consistent with ***** disease. Pathology from the <22>FNA</22> demonstrated <23>moderately differentiated pancreatic adenocarcinoma</23>. <24>ERCP</24> also performed, with placement of a <25>covered metal stent</25>. - 02/19/22: Baseline <26>*****-9</26> markedly elevated at <27>77,736</27> (in the context of <28>obstructive jaundice</28>) Following consultation with both myself and Dr. ***** from our 
