ner_results: ```plaintext
The dose indicators for <1>CT</1> are the volume <2>Computed Tomography Dose Index (CTDIvol)</2> and the <3>Dose Length Product (DLP)</3>, and are measured in units of <4>mGy</4> and <5>mGy-cm</5>, respectively. These indicators are not patient dose, but values generated from the <6>CT scanner</6> acquisition factors. Individual patient doses calculated from these values will depend on patient size and can substantially underestimate or overestimate the actual dose. For additional information on radiation dose, safety, and protection in the <7>UCSF Department of Radiology and Biomedical Imaging</7>, please refer to one of our FAQ leaflets on "<8>Computed Tomography (CT)</8>" or go to *****://*****.*****.*****.*****/*****/*****.  FINDINGS:  No focal consolidations or pleural effusions. Multiple bilateral noncalcified pulmonary nodules, the largest three a <9>4 mm left upper lobe subpleural nodule</9> (series 2, image 69), <10>left upper lobe subpleural 5 mm nodule</10> (series 2, image 73) and <11>5 mm left lower lobe peripheral nodule</11> (series 2, image 161) are noted. A cluster of nodules in the <12>peripheral right middle lobe</12> (series 2, image 176) is likely inflammatory.  Extensive <13>mediastinal lymphadenopathy</13> is noted, including a <14>1.5 cm left pretracheal node</14>, <15>2.3 cm right hilar node</15>, <16>1.9 cm subcarinal node</16> and <17>1 cm AP window node</17>. A <18>9 mm cardiophrenic node</18>, <19>1.4 cm left supraclavicular node</19> and <20>7 mm right internal mammary node</20> are also noted.  Normal heart size. No pericardial effusions.  <21>Diffuse osteopenia</21>. No suspicious osseous lesions.  Please see separate report for <22>CT abdomen pelvis</22>
``` 
