ner_results: ```plaintext
VIDEO VISIT  I performed this consultation using real-time Telehealth tools, including a live video connection between my location and the patient's location. Prior to initiating the consultation, I obtained informed verbal consent to perform this consultation using Telehealth tools and answered all the questions about the Telehealth interaction.      Mr. ***** is a 61 y.o. male whom I am seeing as a video visit at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center for follow-up of his metastatic <1>pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma</1>.    History of Present Illness, reviewed:  This pleasant gentleman initially presented to medical attention in December 2019 with <2>urinary urge incontinence</2> (<3>urinalysis</3> negative), with associated <4>suprapubic discomfort</4>. He also endorsed <5>weight loss</5> of 10 pounds since June, ***** with the beginning of the *****-19 pandemic, but otherwise no <6>fever</6> or <7>infectious symptoms</7>. Ultimately, as his symptoms progressed, he underwent a <8>CT scan of the abdomen/pelvis</8> (01/24/20) that demonstrated a <9>3.5 cm pancreatic tail mass</9> with <10>occlusion of the splenic vein</10> and <11>encasement of the splenic artery</11> with resultant <12>upper abdominal collaterals</12>. Possible <13>abutment of the mass on the stomach</13> and <14>left adrenal gland</14> was seen. Additionally, there was <15>multifocal hepatic metastatic disease</15> with resultant <16>mild right intrahepatic biliary dilatation</16>, as well as <17>diffuse circumferential rectosigmoid wall thickening</17>, possibly representing <18>proctitis</18> for which he received a <19>2 week course of Septra</19> -> <20>ciprofloxacin</20> which provided <21>mild pain relief</21>. <22>Laboratory studies</22> (02/09/2020) showed his <23>liver enzymes</23> to be moderately elevated, with <24>ALT 114</24> and <25>AST 71</25>, and his <26>CA 19-9</26> to be markedly abnormal at <27>19,362 U/mL</27>.    He eventually underwent a <28>CT guided liver biopsy</28> on 02/29/20, and <29>pathology</29> was consistent with <30>adenocarcinoma of pancreatobiliary origin</30>. He also subsequently was found to have <31>cytologically-confirmed peritoneal carcinomatosis</31>.
``` 
