 by ascites for which he received <1>furosemide</1> and <2>spironolactone</2>. He was on the <3>furosemide</3> and <4>spironolactone</4> for 3-4 weeks. lost 30 lbs perioperatively.     08/30/13: <5>CT chest wo contrast</5> showed stable appearance of the chest without <6>pulmonary nodules</6> or <7>lymphadenopathy</7> and an ovoid soft tissue density is seen just inferior to the <8>pancreas</8> on the last ***** of the <9>chest CT</9>.    11/23/2012: <10>Restaging MRI AP</10> showed multiple enhancing lesions in the <11>liver</11> with the largest in the <12>right lobe liver</12> measuring up to <13>10 mm</13> on the <14>arterial phase</14>. The <15>arterial phase imaging</15> was somewhat limited due to <16>portal venous contamination</16>. An attempted <17>ultrasound guided biopsy</17> of the <18>liver lesion</18> seen on the 11/23/2012 <19>MRI</19> but the <20>ultrasound</20> failed to reveal any nodules in the <21>liver</21> that warranted <22>biopsy</22>. A followup <23>triphasic liver CT</23> also did not visualized on the <24>hypervascular lesion</24> seen on the November 24 <25>MRI</25>.     03/04/2013: <26>Restaging MRI AP</26> showed multiple small foci of persistent <27>arterial phase enhancement</27> within the <28>liver</28>, the largest of which measures <29>1.5 cm</29> that is better seen on the current <30>MRI</30>. There is a <31>10 mm nodule</31> at the <32>posterior right hepatic lobe</32> is not well appreciated on current exam. The remainder of the nodules appear stable.     08/30/2013: <33>Restaging MRI AP</33> showed decrease in number of enhancing foci in the <34>liver</34>. <35>Restaging chest CT</35> showed no ***** in the <36>chest</36>.    03/10/2014: <37>Restaging MRI AP</37> showed multiple small arterially enhancing <38>liver lesions</38> are again seen, the largest measuring <39>1.6 cm</39> in the <40>lateral aspect of the right hepatic lobe</40>. A few of these lesions are seen to enhance more prominently on the current study compared to the prior study of 08/30/2013. However, the lesions were even more intensely enhancing and apparently larger on the more remote study of 03/04/2013. It remains unclear if these variations in lesion conspicuity and size are related to fluctuations of true pathology versus differential 
