 to intermediate sized cells with minimal pleomorphism, ample cytoplasm, round to ovoid nuclei, and speckled chromatin. Mitotic count is less than one per 10 *****. There is no necrosis. <1>Immunohistochemical stains</1> performed at the original institution and submitted for our review are interpreted as follows in the <2>tumor cells</2>: - <3>Synaptophysin</3>: Positive. - *****-67 Cannot evaluate (H&E section not submitted for comparison, by report 3%). H&E sections from the <4>retroperitoneal lymph node</4> (*****-*****) shows an infiltrating <5>tumor</5> in a background of fibrovascular tissue. The <6>tumor</6> is composed of trabeculae of intermediate sized cells with occasional ***** formation. The <7>tumor cells</7> show mild atypia, ample cytoplasm, round to ovoid nuclei, and conspicuous nucleoli. Mitotic count is at most 3 per 10 high power fields. There is no necrosis. No prominent nucleoli are seen to suggest a large cell <8>neuroendocrine carcinoma</8>. The amount of cytoplasm argues against a small cell <9>neuroendocrine carcinoma</9> <10>Immunohistochemical stains</10> performed at the original institution and reviewed at ***** show the following results in <11>tumor cells</11>:   - <12>Synaptophysin</12>: Positive.  - *****-67: <13>19.7 % proliferation index</13> (on 2000 cell count).     Overall, the clinical, morphologic and immunohistochemical findings indicate <14>neuroendocrine tumor</14> of <15>pancreatic origin</15> with a possible metastasis to a <16>lymph node</16> (although admittedly no normal nodal tissue is identified in the retroperitoneal biopsy). Based on a <17>pancreatic primary</17>, with <18>3 mitoses / 10 hpf</18>, a <19>proliferation index</19> = <20>19.7%</20>, this would be classified as a <21>WHO grade 2 neuroendocrine tumor</21>.       ASSESSMENT & PLAN    Patient Active Problem List    Diagnosis Date Noted    <22>Primary pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor</22> 09/28/2012     Mr. ***** ***** ***** is a 52 y.o. male who presents