ner_results: ```plaintext
 years. I generally reserve <1>radiation</1> for fit patients who are at particularly high risk of locoregional recurrence (<2>R1 resection</2>, <3>large primary tumor</3>), and suggest waiting until after completion of <4>systemic therapy</4> before considering this modality rather than the 'sandwich' approach. On the whole, for Mr. *****, I do not see any specific features that would warrant offering <5>chemoradiation</5> after he completes his current <6>chemotherapy</6>.    - He was also interested in what additional therapeutic interventions might be offered after completion of his <7>chemotherapy</7>, including clinical trial possibilities. At present, we have none to offer him here at *****. While prior vaccine-based approaches (as ***** following standard adjuvant treatment) have been looked at in this post-resection setting at ***** and ***** *****, without clear evidence of benefit, I am not aware that any such studies are actively ***** at this time. I will contact my colleagues at these institutions to double-check and let the patient know is there is anything he may qualify for.    - Otherwise, we talked about a surveillance strategy after he completes his current treatment, including <8>CT scans</8> and <9>bloodwork</9> at ***** 3-4 month intervals, gradually spacing these surveillance studies out to less frequent intervals as time ***** and his risk of relapse diminishes after the first couple of years. Treatment at the point of relapse would depend on his pattern of recurrence, including <10>radiation</10> (standard or *****) for locoregional-only relapse, vs <11>systemic therapy</11> and possible clinical trials were he to develop evidence of <12>metastatic disease</12>.    All of the patient's questions were answered to his satisfaction. I am certainly happy to see him again at any point in the future at his request or as clinically indicated.    
