ner_results: Attending: ***** *****, MD This is an independent visit History of Present Illness ***** ***** is a 82 y.o. female who presents to our GI <1>Oncology</1> practice at ***** ***** ***** Family Comprehensive Cancer Center for evaluation of metastatic <2>pancreatic adenocarcinoma</2>. History is obtained from the patient as well as from the review of old records. October 2017: Noticed <3>dark stools</3>. Took to local ED and diagnosed with "<4>duodenal ulcer</4>". Had <5>EGD</5>, "treated with <6>laser</6>" to stop <7>bleeding</7>, no further follow-up and imaging at that time. November and December 2016: admitted two more times for <8>nausea</8> and <9>vomiting</9>. 12/28/17 <10>CT Chest, abd, pelvis</10>: <11>Atrophy of the pancreatic body and tail</11> with <12>distention of the duct</12>. An obstructing lesion is not visualized but is suspected. <13>CA 19-9</13>: <14><1</14>, ***** <15>1.8</15>, <16>AFP</16> <17>1.8</17>. 12/31/17: <18>EGD</18>: <19>Severe pyloric stenosis</19> with <20>retained food within the stomach</20> 01/02/18: <21>EGD/EUS</21>: <22>ESOPHAGUS</22> - The mucosa had no <23>ulcers</23> or <24>erosions</24>, <25>strictures</25>, <26>masses</26>, or <27>varices</27>. <28>STOMACH</28> - Some <29>retained food in stomach</29>. This was mostly suctioned out. <30>Pylorus stenosed</30>. Unable to pass <31>endoscope</31> through. Under <32>fluoroscopic guidance</32> a <33>wire</33> was passed through the <34>pylorus</34> into the <35>duodenum</35>. <36>TTS balloon dilation</36> done from <37>10 mm</37> to <38>12 mm</38>. Following this the <39>endoscope</39> could easily be passed into the <40>duodenum</40> <41>DUODENUM</41> - The <42>duodenal bulb antero-inferior wall</42> was <43>edematous</43> with <44>friable mucosa</44>. <45>Multiple biopsies</45> were obtained. The <46>descending duodenum</46> appeared normal. <47>Pathology</47>: <48>duodenal bulb</48>: <49>adenocarcinoma</49> consistent with <50>upper GI/pancreato-biliary primary</50> (full results below under <51>Pathology</51>). 01/05/18: <52>CT chest/abd/pelvis</52> <53>Pancreatic body hypodensity</53> without alteration of <54>pancreatic contour</54> is present with <55>abrupt transition from upstream marked pancreatic duct dilatation to collapsed pancreatic duct</55>, concerning for <56>pancreatic neoplasm</56>. Given the proximity of the <57>pancreatic abnormality</57> in the <58>duodenal stenosis</58>, 
