possibility of <1>neoplastic involvement</1> of the <2>duodenum</2> is considered. Lower abdominal <3>mesenteric irregular spiculated mass</3> adjacent to the <4>sigmoid colon</4> measuring 1.2 x 1 x 2.2 cm is nonspecific but concerning for a <5>neoplastic process</5>. <6>CT Chest W Contrast</6>: Small <7>bilateral pleural effusions</7> with associated <8>lower lobe atelectasis</8>. Abnormal mural thickening involving the <9>distal esophagus</9>, correlate clinically recommend correlation with recent <10>endoscopy</10>. Distended <11>stomach</11> 01/06/18: <12>EGD</12>: <13>ESOPHAGUS</13> - <14>Erosive esophagitis</14>: Grade D -- mucosal breaks extending between folds involving >75% of circumference <15>STOMACH</15> - <16>High grade pyloric stenosis</16>. Abundant fluid in <17>stomach</17> that was suctioned out completely. The <18>endoscope</18> could not be advanced through the <19>strictured pylorus</19>. <20>Balloon dilation</20> was then done by advancing a <21>wire</21> through the <22>pylorus</22> into the <23>duodenum</23> and then serial dilation from 8 mm to 10 mm. The <24>endoscope</24> could then be advanced into the <25>duodenum</25> <26>DUODENUM</26> - <27>ulcerated duodenal bulb</27> as previously noted <28>EUS</28>: Large <29>hypoechoic amorphic mass</29> in the <30>pancreatic head</30> measuring 43 mm x 39 mm. The <31>mass</31> obstructed the <32>main pancreatic duct</32> which was dilated to 11 mm. The <33>mass</33> also infiltrated into the <34>duodenal bulb</34> and there was compression and apparent involvement of the <35>splenic vein/portal vein confluence</35>. It was difficult to determine the extent of involvement of the <36>vessel</36>, and there was flow within the <37>vessel</37>. No obvious <38>mass</38> seen extending into the <39>vessel</39>. 01/08/18: Underwent <40>palliative surgery</40> for <41>gastric outlet obstruction</41>. S/p <42>gastrojejunostomy</42>, <43>cholecystojejunostomy</43>, <44>exploratory laparotomy</44> and <45>biopsy of mesenteric nodules</45> x 3. <46>Pathology</46> from <47>peritoneal/mesentery biopsies</47>: <48>adenocarcinoma</48> c/w <49>upper GI primary</49>. C/b <50>Right arm superficial thrombophlebitis</50> involving both the <51>basilic and cephalic veins</51>, no e/o <52>DVT</52>. 03/18/18: <53>PET/CT</53> 1. Ill-defined <54>FDG avid mass</54> predominantly centered at the <55>duodenum</55> adjacent
