ner_results: Ms. ***** is a 80 y.o. female who returns to our GI Oncology practice at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center for follow-up of her <1>high-grade neuroendocrine tumor</1> of <2>duodenal origin</2>. Oncologic History: To review, the patient initially presented to the Emergency Department at UCSF Medical Center on 01/31/14 with several weeks of worsening <3>crampy abdominal pain</3> after intermittent <4>epigastric pain</4> for one year. Prior outpatient evaluation had been notable of elevated <5>alk phos</5> and <6>AST</6>/<7>ALT</7>. Diagnostic w/u during that UCSF admission included laboratory studies revealing an elevated <8>bilirubin</8> of <9>5.2</9>, and a <10>CT of the abdomen and pelvis</10> that showed a <11>2.3 x 3 CM pancreatic head mass</11>. She underwent <12>ERCP</12> with <13>stent placement</13>, leading to a resolution in her <14>hyperbilirubinemia</14>. Endoscopic findings included a <15>necrotic mucosa</15> presenting as an <16>excavated mass</16> beginning at the inferior angle of the <17>duodenum</17>, causing <18>obstruction</18> and <19>obliteration of the distal bile duct</19>. Biopsies of this lesion showed a <20>grade 3 neuroendocrine tumor</20>, with <21>intermediate grade differentiation</21>. Histologic markers were overall suggestive of a <22>duodenal rather than pancreatic origin</22> for this <23>NET</23>. <24>Octreoscan</24> was obtained, showing only mild uptake in the <25>pancreatic head</25>, and a <26>serum chromogranin A</26> was normal. Ms. ***** was taken for <27>surgical exploration</27> and planned ***** procedure on March 14 given the absence of any <28>metastatic disease</28> on staging studies. Unfortunately, several <29>liver metastases</29> were identified intraoperatively, and surgery was therefore aborted. She underwent repeat <30>ERCP</30> with placement of a <31>permanent metal metallic stent</31> on March 16. Postoperative staging <32>CT-PET</32> showed a stable appearance of her <33>pancreatic mass</33>, and now the presence of several small <34>hepatic lesions</34> c/w <35>metastases</35>. She is now s/p 5 cycles of <36>chemotherapy</36> consisting of the combination of <37>capecitabine</37>/<38>temozolomide</38>. Doses originally consisted of <39>capecitabine 750 mg/m2 BID</39> days November 25. 
