Appropriate affect, alert and oriented. Relevant Diagnostic Studies: Most recent labs notable for the following: (outside labs, 12/18/2012): <1>WBC</1> <2>5.3</2>, <3>Hct</3> <4>36.0</4>, <5>platelets</5> <6>93K</6> <7>Creatinine</7> <8>1.43</8> <9>Glucose</9> <10>329</10> <11>AST</11>/<12>ALT</12> *****/*****, <13>alk phos</13> <14>75</14>, <15>tot bili</15> <16>1.0</16> Most recent imaging notable for the following: <17>CT-PET</17> 12/22/2012: <18>Small, minimally hypermetabolic pulmonary nodules</18> up to <19>1.3 cm</19> in size, <20>R>L</20>. <21>Mild FDG uptake</21> along the <22>CBD</22>. Impression and Recommendations: In summary, Mr. ***** is a <23>79 y.o. male</23> <24>11 months s/p ***** resection of a node-positive pancreatic cancer</24>, followed by adjuvant <25>gemcitabine</25>. We opted not to pursue <26>RT</26> given equivocal data to support its use, plus considerable potential morbidity. He looks and feels well overall, aside from some <27>endocrine and exocrine insufficiency post-surgery/treatment</27>. However, he does have some radiographic findings on ***** ***** *****-PET concerning for possibly <28>pulmonary metastases</28> (the hypermetabolic activity along the CBD is less specific, and not a typical pattern of disease recurrence). For now, I would not immediately embark on resuming treatment, but closely monitor these findings with a repeat scan again in mid-February. In the best case scenario, <29>lung-only metastases</29> can follow a very indolent course and not require treatment for a number of months or even a year or more. At the point of needing to consider systemic therapy, accounting for the patient's age and <30>mild CRI</30>, we could think about <31>capecitabine</31> (+/- <32>oxaliplatin</32>), or possibly <33>gemcitabine</33> in conjunction with *****-<34>paclitaxel</34>. All of the above considerations were explained to the patient and his daughter, who understand and are in agreement. <35>F/u</35> will be arranged with me again in mid- to late- February.
``` 
