This is an independent service.  The available consultant for this service is ***** ***** *****, MD.        PATIENT IDENTIFICATION: Ms. ***** is a 78 y.o. female who returns to our GI Oncology practice at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center for follow-up of her diagnosis of  <1>Metastatic HCC</1>.     ONCOLOGIC HISTORY:   Patient has h/o ***** syndrome and <2>Hep B core positive</2>.     Admitted 11/17/2018 to UCSF for worsening <3>abdominal pain</3> despite increased <4>Norco</4> dosing and <5>obstructive jaundice</5>.     11/17/18: <6>CT</6> showed infiltrating <7>mass</7> centered at distal <8>CBD</8> and adjacent periportal and retroperitoneal ***** with vascular encasement of the <9>SMA</9> and <10>SMV</10> as well as bilat <11>renal arteries</11>.     11/18/18: <12>Chest CT</12> neg for <13>metastatic disease</13>.     11/18/18: <14>ERCP</14> was performed and a <15>metal stent</15> placed in the <16>CBD stricture</16> to relieve the obstruction. <17>EUS core biopsy</17> was done, 1 pass through the <18>pancreatic mass</18> made, one pass through the ***** <19>lymph node</19> made, path confirmed both samples to be <20>pancreatic adenoca</20>, *****.    12/07/18: Patient was reviewed at ***** *****. Consensus was that this was likely a <21>extrahepatic cholangio</21>, unresectable.     12/29/18: <22>C1D1 Gemcitabine/Cisplatin</22>    01/07/19: Presented to ED with <23>constipation</23> x 3 days. <24>CT A/P</24> revealed <25>Moderate amount of solid stool in the rectum</25> suggesting <26>fecal impaction</26>. No evidence for <27>stercoral colitis</27>. There is upstream <28>mild diffuse fluid-filled prominence of the colon</28>. Redemonstrated <29>biliary stent</29> with persistent <30>mild intrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation</30>.    01/12/19: ***** *****/<31>Cis</31>  01/22/19: Patient presents to ED with <32>fever</32>. Workup negative. Fever possibly due to <33>viral illness</33> versus <34>chemo side effect</34>.   01/26/19: ***** *****/<35>Cis</35>  02/09/19: ***** *****/<36>Cis</36> (Final dose)  02/20/19: <37>CT CAP</37> shows <38>stable disease</38>, however, patient was experiencing <39>renal dysfunction</39> probably due to <40>cisplatin</40> and not tolerating therapy well. *****/<41>Cis</41> was discontinued.     Patient was referred to screening for  <42>clinical trials</42> but she didn't have a good option due to the lack of <43>biopsy tissue</43>. We then pursued a management platform based on observation over the holidays for a few months.06/15/2019: <44>CT</44> reveals <45>progression</45> new <46>liver lesions</46>  06/22/2019: Attempted <47>Bx of liver lesion</47>, but no cancer cells seen.
