ner_results: Mr. ***** is a 80 y.o. male who comes to our GI Oncology practice at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center in consultation for his <1>pancreatic head adenocarcinoma</1>. The history was obtained from the patient, along with my review of all his outside records. History of Present Illness This pleasant Chinese-speaking gentleman with multiple medical co-morbidities originally presented in June 2018 with subacute onset of <2>epigastric/RUQ pain</2> and fairly subtle <3>jaundice symptoms</3>. Initial diagnostic <4>w/u</4> at the time included an <5>abdominal U/S</5> (07/23/2018) notable for marked <6>intra and extrahepatic biliary duct dilation</6>, with no obstructing mass identified but a possible <7>choledochal cyst</7>. Subsequent <8>CT scans</8> that same day confirmed <9>biliary dilatation</9>, felt likely 2o to <10>obstructive choledocholithiasis</10>, with associated <11>cholecystitis</11> but again no discrete mass appreciated. <12>MRCP</12> (07/25/2018) failed to visualize a <13>pancreatic head or common bile duct mass</13> at the location of distal common bile duct caliber change; with irregular thickening and enhancement of the <14>gallbladder fundus</14>. <15>ERCP</15> was performed on 07/24/2018, with extraction a <16>CBD stone</16> and placement of two <17>7F x 10 cm stents</17> bilaterally above a high grade <18>stricture</18> in the intrapancreatic portion of the <19>common bile duct</19>. He has since undergone several additional <20>ERCPs</20>, most recently on 09/30/2018, for <21>biliary stent exchanges</21>. Additionally, <22>EUS</22> on 10/08/2018 demonstrated a <23>20 X 16mm irregular and hypoechoic mass</23> in the <24>head of the pancreas</24>, invading into the <25>common bile duct</25>; as well as a <26>hypodensity</26> of the ***** that was suspicious for <27>tumor involvement of the gallbladder</27>. <28>FN biopsy</28> (<29>25G SharkCore needle</29>) confirmed <30>adenocarcinoma</30>. The patient's most recent imaging studies consisted of repeat <31>CT scan</31> performed on 09/17/2018, notable for interval development of <32>4.8 cm mass</32> centered in the <33>gallbladder fundus</33> with <34>hepatic invasion</34> concerning for <35>gallbladder carcinoma</35>; as well. 
