ner_results: ```plaintext
 radiology reports, as follows:     03/21/2021 <1>CT ABDOMEN/PELVIS</1>  FINDINGS:  Lower thorax: <2>Right atrial enlargement</2>.     Liver and biliary tree: <3>Biliary stent</3> from the right hepatic duct into the duodenum. Interval placement of biliary stent with tip in the left hepatic duct with tip in the proximal jejunum. Similar degree of <4>intrahepatic bile ductal dilation</4>, left greater than right. Interval increase in <5>biliary enhancement</5> (for example August 64). Multiple wedge-shaped peripheral areas of <6>hypoattenuation</6>, predominantly of the right hepatic lobe, which are increased/new from prior, the largest in segment 4A/8 (November 42 and August 46). These regions do not persist on delayed imaging however there is some new faint <7>capsular retraction</7>. Some of these areas have small associated <8>lesions</8> which are new, for example the 5 mm lesions in segment October 02 (July 38-40 and 03/12/16). Unchanged <9>hepatic cysts</9>.     Gallbladder: <10>Fundal adenomyomatosis</10>.     Spleen: <11>Punctate calcified granulomas</11>.     Pancreas: Similar <12>atrophy of the pancreatic body and tail</12>. Main pancreatic duct is similarly dilated measuring up to 9 mm with focal tapering at the head the level of the ***** <13>pancreatic mass</13> measuring approximately 2.0 x 2.0 cm (August 98), previously measuring 2.1 x 1.9 cm. The <14>splenic and portal vein</14> are encased by <15>tumor</15>. This mass causes mild <16>portal vein stenosis</16> and moderate to severe <17>splenic vein stenosis</17> (September 070). The <18>splenic artery</18> and <19>proper hepatic artery</19> are encased by tumor with severe <20>stenosis of the proper hepatic</20> (August 92) and moderate narrowing of the <21>splenic artery</21> (July 93). Similar stranding around the <22>SMA</22> and <23>celiac root</23>.     Adrenal glands: Unchanged <24>nodular thickening of the left adrenal gland</24>. The right adrenal gland is normal.     Kidneys and ureters: <25>Subcentimeter renal hypodensities</25> are too small to characterize but are statistically likely to represent <26>renal cysts</26>. <27>Punctate hyperattenuating focus of the right interpolar region</27>,
``` 
