ner_results: ```plaintext
Mr. ***** is a 37 y.o. male who comes to our <1>GI Oncology practice</1> at the <2>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</2> in consultation for his <3>metastatic pancreatic cancer</3>. The history was obtained from the patient, along with my review of all his outside records. History of Present Illness This gentleman first developed <4>nausea</4>/<5>vomiting</5>, waxing/waning, beginning in <6>November 2014</6>, but progressively worsening over time. <7>Labs</7> drawn by his <8>PCP</8> demonstrated an elevated <9>lipase</9>, prompting his presentation to his local <10>ED</10> on <11>01/06/2015</11> for further <12>w/u</12> of presumed <13>pancreatitis</13>. <14>Abdominal U/S</14> showed multiple <15>liver masses</15> consistent with <16>malignancy</16>. Subsequent <17>CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis with contrast</17> showed a <18>6 cm irregular mass</18> in the <19>body of the pancreas</19> with encasement of the <20>hepatic and splenic arteries</20> and invasion of the <21>third portion of the duodenum</21> with <22>obstruction</22>, and multiple <23>hepatic metastases</23>. <24>CT-PET</24> confirmed a <25>pancreatic body primary tumor</25> infiltrating into the <26>left adrenal fossa</26> and to the <27>third portion of the duodenum</27>: <28>6 x 4.6 x 5.2 cm</28>/<29>SUV 5.1</29>; extensive, confluent <30>liver metastases</30>; numerous <31>bilateral pulmonary nodules</31>; at least <32>3 hypermetabolic skeletal metastases</32> (<33>sacrum</33>, <34>mid T5 vertebral body</34>); small volume <35>ascites</35>; <36>omental nodularity</36> and <37>peritoneal thickening</37>; and numerous <38>mesenteric lymph nodes</38>. <39>Spine MRI</39> showed focal signal abnormality in the <40>S1 vertebral body</40>, and areas of <41>disc protrusion</41> at <42>T12-L1</42> and <43>L3-4</43>. During the patient's hospital admission, he was maintained on a <44>liquid diet</44> and <45>rx'ed with anti-emetics</45>. <46>Liver biopsy</46> was performed on <47>01/08/2015</47>, which apparently showed <48>metastatic moderately differentiated adenocarcinoma of pancreatic or biliary origin</48> (formal report N/A). The patient's present review of systems was reviewed and notable for the following: - <49>PO intake</49> modest, including soups and some solids. No/minimal postprandial <50>vomiting</50>; <51>nausea</51> under reasonable control on <52>anti-emetics</52> -
``` 
