ner_results: Ms. ***** is a 56 y.o. female who returns to our GI Oncology practice at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center for follow-up of her metastatic <1>nonfunctioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor</1>. Previous treatment has included a <2>distal pancreatectomy</2> of her primary lesion back in 2002; several subsequent <3>radiofrequency ablations</3> for <4>liver confined disease</4>; and a short period of <5>Sandostatin</5> that was poorly tolerated with significant <6>GI symptoms</6>. We had also been considering the possibility of <7>liver-directed embolization</7> (***** or <8>Y-90</8>) of her slowly growing <9>hepatic metastases</9>, but due to her vascular anatomy post-resection, she was determined not to be a candidate for this. More recently, Dr. ***** ***** took her back to the <10>OR</10> in September 2011 for planned repeat laparoscopic <11>RFA</11>, but found such significant adhesions of <12>small bowel</12> to <13>liver</13> and <14>abdominal wall</14> that he could not safely dissect the anterior surface of the liver to perform the ablation. The patient has since started systemic therapy with <15>everolimus</15>, an oral <16>mTOR inhibitor</16>, beginning in October 2011. Serial <17>CT scans</17> since then have demonstrated essentially stable hepatic metastases (see most recent results, below). Due to some <18>gastrointestinal issues</18> with the everolimus affecting her daily *****, her dose was decreased from <19>10 mg</19> to <20>7.5 mg</20> daily beginning in April 2012, which she has been handling well overall. On present review of systems, the patient notes: - Admitted for <21>partial SBO</21> at the end of May x 5 days, managed conservatively with <22>NG tube decompression</22>; since then, she has been doing fine (gradually advancing diet, now on <23>low-fiber diet</23>). Everolimus held during that period of time, resumed two weeks ago. - <24>Bowel movements</24> have returned to baseline (currently 1-2x/day; better than previous more frequent BMs). - <25>Energy level</25> is fine, working and exercising. - No <26>polyuria</26>. - No significant localizing areas of <27>pain</27> at present; does not. 
