<1>Pancreatic duct dilatation</1> in the <2>residual body</2> and <3>tail</3>.  <4>Soft tissue density</4> demonstrated posterior to the <5>proximal SMA</5> with partial encasement measuring <6>1.5 cm</6>. Interval appearance of an <7>ill-defined sclerotic density</7> within the <8>L2 vertebral body</8> when compared to <9>08/31/2011 scans</9>.      ASSESSMENT & PLAN  In summary, ***** ***** ***** is a <10>69 y.o. female</10> who presents to our <11>GI Oncology practice</11> at the <12>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</12> for evaluation of her <13>recurrent pancreatic adenocarcinoma</13>. Following her original ***** operation, she received <14>adjuvant gemcitabine</14>, mostly dosed in a somewhat unusual fashion of <15>once-monthly</15>. She now has evidence of <16>recurrent disease</16> based on suspicious <17>radiographic findings</17> (<18>local recurrence</18> plus <19>presumed metastatic disease</19> to the <20>lymph nodes</20> and <21>L2 vertebral body</21>) and a rising <22>*****-9 biomarker</22>. Apparently the patient had a repeat <23>PET/CT</23> obtained <24>last week</24>, while <25>off treatment</25> for the <26>past month-plus</26>, although we currently do not have the report or the images available for review. We will work on obtaining these as well as getting a full set of <27>laboratory studies</27> during patient's visit today.     Her ***** <28>off-treatment</28> is reasonably good, and depending on the extent and progression of her disease we will decide on the need and timing of re-initiating <29>chemotherapy</29>. The patient understands that such treatment would be for <30>prolongation of life</30> (but not <31>cure</31>) and possibly <32>symptom palliation</32>. As the patient has concerns about <33>chemotherapy</33> and the <34>side effects</34> related to <35>systemic therapy</35>, our recommendation would be try treatment with ***** or ***** and see how well she tolerates it. We reviewed some of the potential <36>side effects</36> including but not limited to : <37>bone marrow suppression</37> increasing the risk of <38>infections</38>, <39>bleeding</39> and <40>transfusions</40>, <41>nausea</41>, <42>vomiting</42>, <43>diarrhea</43>, <44>cold sensitivity</44>, <45>mucositis</45>, <46>neuropathy</46>, and <47>hand-foot syndrome</47>. We provided the patient and his daughter to ask 
