Mr. ***** is a 64 y.o. male who comes to our <1>GI Oncology</1> practice at the <2>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</2> in consultation for his resected <3>pancreatic cancer</3>. The history was obtained from the patient, along with my review of all his outside records. History of Present Illness This pleasant gentleman is from ***** ***** has received all of his care to date there. He initially presented last March with <4>weight loss</4>, <5>nausea</5>, <6>abdominal pain</6>, and <7>scleral icterus</7> and was found on laboratory tests to have elevated <8>LFTs</8>, including a <9>total bilirubin</9> of <10>7.4</10>, <11>AST</11>/<12>ALT</12> of <13>624</13> and <14>661</14> respectively, and an <15>alk phos</15> of <16>447</16>. Imaging studies included <17>MRCP</17> (04/18/2017), showing a focal mass lesion in head and uncinate process of the <18>pancreas</18> (<19>46x26 mm</19>) obstructing the lower end intrapancreatic portion of <20>CBD</20> and <21>MPD</21>; and <22>CT scan</22> (04/21/2017) confirming a <23>3.3x2.5x3.8 cm mass</23> in the <24>pancreatic head</24> causing both <25>pancreatic</25> and <26>intrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation</26>, with suggestion of <27>SMV invasion</27>. He was taken directly to surgery on 04/24/2017, consisting of a <28>classical pancreaticoduodenectomy</28> (*****) on 04/24/2017. Final pathology revealed a <29>2.2x2.1x2 cm moderately differentiated adenocarcinoma</29> with negative margins and <30>0 of 9 involved LNs</30>. Post-operative course was notable for an <31>intraabdominal abscess</31> requiring <32>percutaneous drainage</32>. He was eventually discharged home on 05/15/2017, tolerating limited oral nutrition (some rice and soup) but also receiving supplemental feeds via an <33>enteral feeding tube</33> (<34>600ml/day</34>). He was eventually able to start on <35>adjuvant chemotherapy</35> consisting of single-agent <36>gemcitabine</36> on 06/11/2017 originally intended to be given on a <37>three-week-on, one-week-off dosing schedule</37>; however, this was subsequently switched to a <38>two-week-on, one-week-off dosing schedule</38> due to <39>cytopenias</39> and for better tolerability. He has now completed <40>5 cycles</40>, with his most recent dose in ***** administered on
``` 
