ner_results: Mr. ***** is a 70 y.o. male who returns to our GI Oncology practice at the <1>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</1> for follow-up of his <2>metastatic pancreatic cancer</2>, originally diagnosed in <3>March 2013</3> after presenting with <4>progressive abdominal discomfort</4>. <5>CT scan</5> at that time demonstrated a <6>3.9 cm pancreatic tail necrotic mass</6>, extending into the <7>splenic hilum</7> and abutting the <8>stomach</8>, with surrounding stranding in the fat which extends toward the <9>L kidney</9> and <10>splenic flexure of the colon</10>; with encasement of the <11>splenic artery</11>. Also noted were adjacent <12>LNs 9 mm in size</12>; <13>cystic liver lesions up to 3.6 cm in size</13>; <14>small free fluid in the pelvis</14> with <15>pelvic/peritoneal nodules up to 1.4 cm in size</15>; <16>nodular thickening along greater curvature of the stomach</16>; and <17>L ureteral obstruction</17> <18>2o to a 2.3 cm retroperitoneal nodule</18> causing <19>moderate hydronephrosis</19>. <20>Endoscopic FNA of the pancreatic mass</20> on <21>03/27/2013</21> confirmed <22>adenocarcinoma</22>. The patient underwent <23>ureteral stent placement</23> on <24>03/28/2013</24>. Initial treatment consisted of *****, to which he responded well with interval imaging demonstrating reduction in both his <25>primary tumor</25> and measurable distant sites of disease. However, he did develop some cumulative <26>cytopenias</26> and <27>peripheral sensory neuropathy</27> ultimately requiring holding of the <28>oxaliplatin</28> beginning with <29>cycle #6</29>. He then continued with ***** alone, with an eventual spacing of his dosing schedule to <30>q3 weeks</30>. Between the ***** and the *****, he completed <31>18 cycles</31> in total through <32>February 2014</32>. Following his initial consultation with me in <33>February 2014</33>, I suggested he meet Dr. ***** of our Surgical faculty practice to discuss his surgical options, given the absence of measurable metastatic disease on his most recent <34>CT scans</34> and excellent durable disease control. Repeat imaging here at ***** on <35>05/22/2014</35> confirmed only the presence of an <36>ill-defined hypodense pancreatic tail mass</36> measuring approximately <37>2.0</37>. 
