This is an independent visit    PATIENT IDENTIFICATION: Ms. ***** is a 61 y.o. female who returns to our GI Oncology practice at the <1>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</1> for follow-up of her diagnosis of <2>pancreas adenocarcinoma metastatic to liver</2> currently participating in *****#***** <3>Nivolumab</3>/<4>Abraxane</4>/<5>Gemcitabine</5> ***** clinical trial.     ONCOLOGIC HISTORY:   She initially presented in November 2017 with what was thought to be <6>gallstone pancreatitis</6>. She underwent a <7>cholecystectomy</7>. Following this, she had recurrent <8>pancreatitis</8> and <9>obstructive jaundice</9> requiring a <10>stent</10>. Despite repeated imaging with <11>ultrasound</11>, no pancreatic abnormalities were identified.     On 02/25/2018 ***** ***** revealed a <12>mass in the head of the pancreas</12>. She underwent a <13>core and fine-needle aspirate</13> of the mass and this showed <14>adenocarcinoma</14>. She was apparently thought to be a candidate for <15>resection</15>. Prior to resection, <16>CT</16>/<17>PET</17> was done on 02/28/18. The CT suggested a possible <18>cyst in the left lobe of the liver</18> and a follow up PET suggested this was <19>PET avid</19>. She was told she had <20>metastatic disease</20> and has been scheduled for treatment with <21>gemcitabine</21> and <22>Abraxane</22>.    03/21/18: Patient had initial consultation with Dr. *****. We were hopeful that the PET was a false positive. An <23>MRI</23> to further evaluate the liver was recommended.     03/24/18: MRI showed <24>multiple liver lesions</24>, with rapid progression compared with 02/28/2018.    03/29/18: Signed informed consent for the ***** trial *****#*****
``` 
