5 mm. New focus of low attenuation within the <1>pancreatic tail</1> measures <2>7 mm</2>, which may represent a dilated side branch. The <3>mass</3> continues to encircle the <4>SMV</4> and <5>splenic vein</5> at the <6>portal splenic confluence</6>, though the degree of mass effect is decreased from prior and these vessels now demonstrate patency. <7>Main portal vein</7> remains patent. The mass abuts the anterior aspect of the proximal <8>SMA</8>. Soft tissue stranding encircles the proximal <9>celiac axis</9>, unchanged from prior. Adrenal Glands: Unremarkable Kidneys: <10>Bilateral renal cysts</10> measuring up to <11>4.7 cm</11>. GI Tract: Unremarkable Vasculature: Mass involvement of the adjacent vasculature as described above. <12>Gastric varices</12>. The <13>hepatic artery</13> arises from the SMA. Lymphadenopathy: Few scattered small <14>mesenteric nodes</14> measuring up to <15>7 mm</15>. Ascites: Absent Bladder: Unremarkable Reproductive organs: Unremarkable Extraperitoneal soft tissues: Unremarkable Lines/drains/medical devices: None IMPRESSION: 1. Compared to 11/07/2016, interval decrease in size of <16>pancreatic mass</16> now measuring up to <17>5.5 cm</17> (previously <18>6.6 cm</18>), with decreased mass effect on the portal splenic confluence and proximal SMV. There is persistent encasement of the portal splenic confluence and proximal SMV, abutment of the proximal SMA, and soft tissue stranding encasing the celiac axis and <19>splenic artery</19>. 2. Unchanged indeterminate <20>hypoattenuating lesions</20> in the <21>liver</21> measuring up to <22>10 mm</22> in segment 2. <23>CT chest</23> 01/20/17 LUNGS: The <24>lungs</24> are clear. PLEURA: The <25>pleura</25> is normal. MEDIASTINUM: <26>7 mm right anterior diaphragmatic lymph node</26> is unchanged. No new intrathoracic lymphadenopathy. HEART/***** *****: Normal <27>heart size</27>. No <28>pericardial effusion</28>. <29>Thoracic aorta</29> and <30>pulmonary artery</30> are normal caliber. BONES/SOFT TISSUES: A few small <31>sclerotic foci</31> within <32>ribs</32>, most notable in the <33>right lateral seventh rib</33> (image 147,
