Mr. ***** is a 63 y.o. male who returns to our <1>GI Oncology</1> practice at the <2>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</2> for follow-up of his <3>metastatic pancreatic cancer</3>, <4>BRCA2-associated</4>, currently receiving <5>salvage chemotherapy</5> with *****. He had previously been enrolled in our <6>phase I clinical trial</6> evaluating the combination of ***** plus the <7>oral hedgehog inhibitor IPI-926</7>; we ultimately had to take him off treatment after <8>6 cycles</8> (and two dose reductions in the *****), despite evidence of good disease control, due to significant cumulative <9>fatigue</9>. He subsequently resumed therapy consisting of reduced-dose biweekly ***** in December 2011, and completed <10>12 cycles</10> in total with excellent disease control. Following a <11>chemotherapy holiday</11> since June 2012, he demonstrated radiographic evidence of further <12>disease progression</12> (<13>locoregional recurrence</13> in the <14>pancreatic tail</14>, plus <15>nodal and mesenteric implants</15>), on which basis we resumed him on ***** in November. He received <16>6 cycles</16>, well-tolerated overall; however, <17>CT scan</17> on <18>02/20/2013</18> demonstrated modest <19>disease progression</19>, including a new <20>right hepatic 1.1 cm lesion</20> and increased <21>soft tissue density</21> around the <22>celiac artery</22> and enlargement of <23>retroperitoneal lymph nodes</23>. On this basis, we elected to switch him to the combination of <24>gemcitabine</24> plus *****-<25>paclitaxel</25> on an every-other-week dosing schedule (days 1 and 15 in 28-day cycles). He has now completed <26>3 full cycles</26> of treatment. On present review of systems, the patient notes that he has been tolerating current treatment very well, with mild <27>fatigue</27>, <28>flu-like symptoms</28> and <29>skeletal pains</29> that last 3-4 days following each <30>chemotherapy infusion</30>. He reports a good appetite with stable to increasing weight. He is less active than previously although thinks his <31>energy level</31> remains fairly good. He denies any <32>diarrhea</32>. He has some mild chronic <33>L-sided abdominal discomfort</33>. He does have treatment-related
