Mr. ***** is a 69 y.o. male whom I am seeing at our GI Oncology practice at the <1>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</1> in follow up for his resected <2>ampullary cancer</2>.     History of Present Illness, reviewed:  This pleasant gentleman's oncologic history is as follows:     March 2021: Developed <3>diarrhea</3> and <4>obstructive jaundice</4> symptoms shortly after undergoing <5>arthroscopic knee surgery</5>   04/20/2021: Presented to local <6>ED</6> and admitted with <7>abdominal pain</7> and <8>jaundice</8>. <9>Total bilirubin</9> 6.1 on admission.   <10>Abdominal U/S</10> and <11>CT scan</11> (04/20/2021) notable for moderate dilatation of the intra and extrahepatic <12>biliary ducts</12>, dilated <13>CBD</13> tapering at the <14>pancreatic head</14>; no discrete mass appreciated   <15>ERCP</15> (04/20/2021): Abnormal <16>ampulla</16> noted. <17>Pancreatic duct</17> was 2.8 mm in the body and 4.2 mm in the head. No obvious mass lesion. <18>Bile ducts</18> upstream were dilated, as well as the <19>gallbladder</19>. The <20>common bile duct</20> measured 15 mm. A distal narrowing with a 1 cm stricture was documented. Thickened duct with upstream dilatation. Underwent <21>sphincterotomy</21>, <22>balloon sweep</22>. <23>Biliary stent</23> placed. <24>Duct brushings pathology</24> noted as atypical, favored to represent reactive/degenerative change. Patient did develop some post-procedural <25>pancreatitis</25>.   05/02/2021: Consult with Dr. ***** ***** (<26>UCSF Surg Onc</26>); index of suspicion high of a <27>ampullary carcinoma</27>, recommended ***** procedure, which was performed on 05/16/2021 at ***** ***** Hospital. Final pathology:  -----------------  <28>Tumor Site</28>:  Intra-ampullary: <29>Ampullary ductal</29>   (pancreaticobiliary-type)   <30>Histologic Type</30>:  <31>Adenocarcinoma</31>, pancreaticobiliary type   <32>Histologic Grade</32>:  G2 Moderately differentiated (50% to 95% of tumor   composed of glands)   <33>Tumor Size</33>:  3.2 cm   <34>Extent Of Tumor</34>:  Invades into <35>duodenal submucosa</35> and <36>muscularis propria</36>, extends more than 0.5 cm into <37>pancreas</37> and extends into   <38>peripancreatic soft tissue</38>   <39>Margins</39>: All margins negative for invasive
``` 
