Mr. ***** is a 66 y.o. male who returns to our <1>GI Oncology practice</1> at the <2>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</2> for follow-up of his <3>metastatic pancreatic cancer</3>. To review briefly, the patient was originally diagnosed back in March 2012 with <4>borderline resectable disease</4> and initiated <5>chemotherapy</5> with ***** on <6>04/20/2012</6>. Shortly after completion of his first treatment cycle he developed <7>obstructive jaundice</7>, for which he was hospitalized in ***** and underwent <8>MRCP</8> and <9>ERCP</9> on <10>04/29/2012</10> with placement of an ***** <11>stent</11>. His <12>LFTs</12> improved sufficiently to allow him to receive his 2nd cycle without <13>irinotecan</13> (*****) on <14>05/20/12</14>. The patient then required a repeat <15>ERCP</15> on <16>06/03/12</16> secondary to <17>persistent hyperbilirubinemia</17>; <18>biliary obstruction</18> was noted in the distal bile duct and his <19>stent</19> was exchanged for two plastic stents. Thereafter, his <20>laboratory parameters</20> improved and he symptomatically felt much better, and was able to resume ***** at full doses for cycles #3 and 4. Starting with cycle #5, ***** was dose reduced by 20-25% for better tolerability. Following this fifth cycle, he was admitted to the ***** of the <21>Valley Hospital</21> on *****/*****-*****/*****/***** with <22>fevers</22>, <23>diarrhea</23>, and <24>dehydration</24>, for which he was treated with <25>Levaquin</25> and also underwent <26>ERCP</26> on <27>07/29/12</27> with <28>stent exchange</28>. Following recovery, he received one final cycle of ***** alone in <29>August 2012</29>. He was subsequently taken to <30>surgery</30> here on <31>09/07/2012</31>, where he was found to have a <32>firm mass</32> in the <33>head of pancreas</33>, closely adherent to but not invading the <34>SMV</34> and able to be separated. A successful ***** <35>operation</35> was performed, with final <36>surgical pathology</36> revealing a ***** <37>tumor</37> with direct invasion into multiple structures, negative margins, +<38>perineural invasion</38>, and <39>0/14 LNs positive</39>. The patient's postoperative course was notable for a bout of <40>C difficile colitis</40>, but he was well enough to be discharged to home on
``` 
