Subjective:     He presented in December 2014. <1>CT scan</1>, performed at ***** for <2>abdominal and back pain</2>, demonstrated a <3>3.2 x 2.2 mass</3> in the body of the <4>pancreas</4> with partial encasement of the <5>SMV</5>, <6>splenic vein</6>, and <7>splenic portal confluence</7>.  Several borderline prominent <8>retroperitoneal lymph nodes</8> were identified.  There were no focal <9>hepatic or bony lesions</9>, or other evidence of distant <10>metastatic disease</10>.  <11>Tissue biopsy</11> obtained during <12>EUS</12> confirmed <13>adenocarcinoma</13>.    The patient was consented to receive first-line therapy for his <14>borderline resectable disease</14> via the *****# ***** clinical trial protocol studying ***** (<15>pegylated recombinant human hyaluronidase</15>) with <16>chemotherapy</16>.  After a ***** ***** *****-in period with ***** on Days 1 and 4, <17>Cycle 1 Day 1</17> and <18>Day 8 infusions</18> of *****, <19>*****-paclitaxel</19> and <20>gemcitabine</20> were given on <21>February 10</21> & *****/*****.  <22>Day 15 infusions</22> were held on <23>02/23/2015</23> for <24>CTCAE grade 3 neutropenia</24> and <25>thrombocytopenia</25>.    <26>Cycle 2, Day 1 infusions</26> of all 3 study agents at full dose were given on <27>03/03/2015</27>.  The patient self-administered <28>Neupogen</28> on <29>Days 3 and 4</29>, resulting in <30>severe spine and rib pain</30>, <31>nausea</31> and <32>fatigue</32> shortly after the 2nd injection.  He stayed in bed the remainder of the day and did not eat or drink.  He was seen in the ***** ***** on <33>03/07/2015</33> and received <34>IV hydration</34>, <35>ondansetron</35> and <36>hydromorphone</36> with improvement in symptoms.    <37>Cycle 2, day 8 infusions</37> were better tolerated. He did experience some <38>esophageal spasm</38> and also developed <39>thrush</39> which was treated with <40>fluconazole</40>. He had excessive <41>bone pain</41> with <42>Neupogen</42> but this was managed with <43>Claritin</43>. He's had recurrent <44>cramping</44>, <45>grade 2</45>, which has been managed with intermittent <46>Decadron</46>. On <47>day 21 of cycle 2</47>, he required <48>IV fluids</48>. He was noted to be <49>confused</49> but it was felt this might have been due to <50>lorazepam</50>. He had sharp increase in <51>pain in his right upper quadrant tenderness</51> resulted in the trip to the <52>ED</52>.
