ner_results: ```plaintext
 today:  -<1>gemcitabine</1> 800 mg/m2 on days 1 and 8 (decreased due to low <2>ANC</2>)  -<3>capecitabine</3> 1500 mg po qam and 2000 mg po qpm on days January 22  cycle is 21 days    -continue <4>Neupogen</4> 480 mcg for 3 days after infusion to help with <5>neutropenia</5>. If he does not feel well after cycle 8, we will not proceed with cycle 9. Otherwise, he will proceed with cycle 9 and will leave for his trip on 06/13/17.    -Monthly <6>CA 19-9</6> levels have been <7>wnl</7> range. We will obtain <8>CT chest</8>, <9>abdomen</9> and <10>pelvis</10> after the completion of <11>adjuvant therapy</11>.    -After the <12>adjuvant therapy</12> has been completed, the patient's disease can be monitored with surveillance <13>CT scans</13> and <14>CA 19-9</14> every 3 months.     -<15>Cancer Genetics</15> appointment was on December 08; results of the <16>genetic testing</16> are negative.    #<17>Prostate cancer</17>: High-risk, September 29 cores positive, ***** 5+4. He established care with Dr. ***** on February 28 and started on <18>ADT therapy</18> with ***** on 02/28/17.   -Plan for <19>surgical resection</19> in September.    #Supportive care: Patient has supply of <20>antiemetics</20>. I recommended that he continue to stay active and maintain his weight while undergoing therapy.     #Goals of care: Curative    #Follow-up: Patient will return to our clinic following his restaging scans via telemedicine visit. As always, we reminded the patient to call us with any questions or concerns in the meantime.    I spent a total of 25 minutes face-to-face with the patient and 25 minutes of that time was spent counseling regarding the treatment plan, medication risks, lifestyle modification and symptoms.      ***** *****, MD, MS  Clinical Assistant Professor of Medicine  Department of Medicine, Division of Oncology   UCSF ***** ***** Family Comprehensive Cancer Center  05/12/2017       
``` 
