ner_results: ```plaintext
Dr. ***** is a 66 y.o. male who comes to our GI Oncology practice at the UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center in consultation for his <1>metastatic pancreatic cancer</1>. The history was obtained from the patient, along with my review of all his outside records. History of Present Illness This pleasant gentleman first noted <2>upper abdominal discomfort</2> radiating to his <3>back</3> in early January, which lasted several days to weeks. ***** provided some relief, and he suspected these symptoms may have represented <4>alcoholic pancreatitis</4> as his <5>alcohol consumption</5> had increased around that time. After he stopped drinking completely, the <6>pain</6> eventually subsided. He has had previous episodes of similar pain lasting a couple days and also subsiding after cessation of alcohol use. During this most recent episode, he saw his gastroenterologist Dr. ***** ***** who ordered a <7>CT abdomen</7> (01/26/20) that showed a <8>3.3 x 3.1 x 2.2 cystic mass</8> in the <9>tail</9> with <10>surrounding fat stranding</10>, with extension of the mass beyond the margin of the <11>pancreatic parenchyma</11>, where it abutted the undersurface of the <12>gastric body</12>. This was felt to be possibly c/w a <13>peripancreatic fluid collection/pseudocyst</13> in the setting of <14>pancreatitis</14>, vs a <15>pancreatic cystic neoplasm</15>. <16>F/u MRI abdomen</16> (03/22/20) showed a <17>4.1 x 2.8 cm unilocular cystic lesion</17> in the <18>pancreatic tail</18> with a <19>5 mm mural nodule</19>, at which time <20>surgical consultation</20> was recommended. He was seen by Dr. ***** ***** of our Surgical faculty practice at ***** on March 27, who ordered <21>repeat imaging</21> (<22>CT pancreas protocol</22>). <23>CT C/A/P</23> (04/13/20) showed persistent visualization of the <24>cystic mass</24> within the <25>pancreatic tail</25>, as well as an <26>indeterminate hypodense lesion</26> in the <27>posterior right hepatic lobe</27>. This was followed by a <28>PET-CT</28> (04/28/20) confirming a <29>complex cystic and solid mass</29> in the <30>tail of the pancreas</30> (approximately <31>3 x 2 cm</31>) with <32>SUV 9.1</32>, with features consistent with a <33>primary pancreatic</33>
``` 
