ner_results: ```plaintext
Mr. ***** is a 42 y.o. male who comes to our <1>GI Oncology practice</1> at the <2>UCSF Comprehensive Cancer Center</2> in consultation for his newly diagnosed <3>metastatic pancreatic cancer</3>. The history was obtained from the patient, along with my review of all his outside records. History of Present Illness This pleasant gentleman, previously entirely healthy, first noted <4>diffuse abdominal discomfort</4> a couple of months ago, with some accompanying <5>loss of appetite</5> and <6>10-lb weight loss</6>. These <7>symptoms</7> gradually progressed, prompting <8>diagnostic w/u</8> to be undertaken, including the following: - <9>09/21/2015</9>: <10>3.3 x 2.9 x 3.3 cm pancreatic head mass</10> with associated <11>pancreatic duct dilatation</11>; <12>innumerable hepatic lesions</12> up to <13>4 cm</13> in size, some with small areas of probable <14>internal hemorrhage</14> with compression of <15>IVC</15> and <16>right renal artery and vein</16> - <17>09/23/2015</17>: <18>EGD/EUS</18>: <19>Large 32.2 x 39.7 mm size pancreatic head mass</19>, associated with <20>small to large liver lesions</20> consistent with <21>metastatic disease</21> (*****, *****, <22>M1</22>). <23>FNA</23> of both <24>pancreatic mass</24> and <25>one liver lesion</25> c/w <26>adenocarcinoma</26>. The patient's present review of systems was reviewed and notable for the following: - <27>35-40 lb weight loss</27> in total - <28>Intermittent diarrhea</28> - <29>Some nausea</29> over past several days; <30>poor appetite</30> - <31>Generalized fatigue</31>; <32>ECOG PS 1</32> - <33>Generalized abdominal discomfort</33>; not requiring any <34>pain medications</34> - (+) <35>night sweats</35> - No <36>jaundice</36> - No <37>HAs</37> or <38>peripheral sensory neuropathy</38> - Normal <39>urination</39> - No <40>SOB</40>/<41>respiratory difficulties</41> - Some <42>reactive depression</42>. The remainder of a full review of systems was negative. Past medical history No chronic medical conditions. S/p <43>bilateral inguinal hernia repair</43> and <44>varicocele repair</44> in childhood. Outpatient Encounter Prescriptions as of <45>10/04/2015</45> Medication Sig Dispense Refill valACYclovir (<46>VALTREX</46>) <47>1 g tablet</47> Take <48>1,000 mg</48> by mouth every <49>12 (twelve) hours</49>.
``` 
