 that we may have to repeat these periodically as needed for comfort. I did also instruct her to restart her <1>diuretic</1> if she can tolerate.    - Recheck <2>labs</2> today, including <3>LFTs</3>.    - Urgent referral to <4>GI</4> for <5>ERCP</5>/***** <6>stent placement</6> for <7>biliary decompression</7>.    - Once <8>LFTs</8> are trending downward (do not need to completely normalize) post-procedure, I again reinforced my recommendation that she start <9>chemotherapy</9>, of which I would favor ***** as the best option in this clinical setting. While she has previously been reluctant to pursue this, she was agreeable at this point to move ahead. We reviewed the logistics, scheduling, need for <10>Mediport placement</10> (for which I will place referral to <11>I.R</11>), and some of the potential side effects of this regimen including but not limited to <12>nausea</12>/<13>vomiting</13>, <14>alopecia</14>, <15>fatigue</15>, <16>cytopenias</16> with resultant <17>immunosuppression</17> and/or <18>anemia</18>, and <19>peripheral sensory neuropathy</19>/<20>cold sensitivity</20>.     - Of note, review of her <21>Foundation One profiling</21> results show, as perhaps her most actionable genetic alteration, an ***** <22>mutation</22>. We do have an ***** ***** <23>inhibitor trial</23> for *****-mutated ***** that we anticipate opening at ***** sometime in the next few months that may represent another option for her in the future, but I am concerned whether or not she would qualify on the basis of concurrent (albeit indolent) <24>PNET</24>.    I spent a total of 40 minutes face-to-face with the patient and her husband, of which greater than 50% was spent in counseling regarding her <25>cancer diagnosis</25>. We will arrange for <26>anti-emetic prescriptions</26> and a <27>chemotherapy teaching session</27> with one of our practice <28>RNs</28> in anticipation of starting treatment ASAP.    
